Following up on the topic of personal and household financial management

```ninjas
finanzas-personales | Finanzas Personales | Es importante para cualquier profesional tener sus finanzas ordenadas y controlar sus gastos. En este artículo mostraré algunas estrategias y consejos para poder tener una saludable situación financiera. | /blog/finanzas-personales/cover.webp
```

I created this small _Livebook_ that lets you manage household finances
using _SQLite3_, _Elixir_, and _Livebook_.

First we have a table of periodic transactions.
It stores any transaction that must be performed once a specific
number of days has passed. For example, paying the electricity bill
every 30 days.

These transactions will be added to the list of daily transactions
at the appropriate moment.

Note also that the amount is an integer value,
since storing money values as decimals is not recommended
because of the computers' discrete floating-point capacity.

Income will be type 0 (type 0) and expenses type 1 (type 1)

```sql
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS periodic_txs (
      id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
      label TEXT NOT NULL,
      amount INTEGER NOT NULL, -- minimum currency value
      type INTEGER NOT NULL, -- 0 income, 1 expense
      days INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,
      created_at TIMESTAMP NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
      applied_at TIMESTAMP NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,

      UNIQUE(label)
    );
```

The following is the table of daily transactions; this table
will store the income and expense transactions of each day.

Every time an economic movement occurs, a record
will be stored in this table.

For example, buying a kilo of bread a day is `$ CLP 1300` and
it will be considered an expense.


```sql
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS txs (
      id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
      amount INTEGER NOT NULL,
      type INTEGER NOT NULL, -- 0 income, 1 expense
      description TEXT,
      created_at TIMESTAMP NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
    )
```

Once a day you must check whether there are periodic income or expenses
whose date has come due and must be applied again.

To verify, we first query the ones that meet the condition and then add them
to the corresponding table, afterwards updating their last-use
date.

```elixir
Exqlite.query!(conn, """
  SELECT * FROM periodic_txs WHERE
  julianday('now') - julianday(applied_at) >= days
""")
|> then(& &1.rows)
|> Enum.each(fn [id, label, amount, type, _days, _created_at, _applied_at] ->
  Exqlite.query!(
    conn,
    """
      INSERT OR IGNORE INTO txs (amount, type, description) VALUES (?, ?, ?)
    """,
    [amount, type, label]
  )

  Exqlite.query!(
    conn,
    """
      UPDATE periodic_txs SET applied_at = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP WHERE id = ?
    """,
    [id]
  )
end)
```

To add daily transactions a simple insertion query is all that is needed.

```elixir
[
  %{
    description: "Cafe de una sirena",
    # expense
    type: 1,
    amount: 5 * 100
  },
  %{
    description: "10 billetes en el sofá",
    # income
    type: 0,
    amount: 10 * 100
  }
]
|> Enum.map(fn item ->
  Exqlite.query!(
    conn,
    """
    INSERT OR IGNORE INTO txs (amount, type, description)
      VALUES (?, ?, ?)
    """,
    [item.amount, item.type, item.description]
  )
end)
```

Finally we can run _SQL_ queries and find out important facts
about our economic behavior.

For example, this is how we get the transactions made
during the current month.


```sql
SELECT SUM(amount) AS total_amount
            FROM   txs
            WHERE created_at BETWEEN
                datetime('now', 'start of month') AND
                datetime('now', 'start of month', '+1 month', '-1 day')
```

